{"file_path":"/home/john/mynotes/shadow_frontier/catalog/ghost_catalog.tex","new_string":"the automaton's, not yours, and (the\n$(3/4)^D$ law) a random message's orbit takes a letter $b\\ge3$ within\na handful of steps of being left to itself; try to compose a message\nwhose forced continuation survives long --- that is exactly the\nchampion-hunting problem, and the house always wins.\n\nWe played the game (\\texttt{search/message\\_champions.py}). Over the\n$44{,}000$-word English dictionary the champion is \\texttt{bopped}:\n$48$ scripted letters, then the cascade composes \\emph{$36$ more} on\nits own (integer $1769681963780500439019$); runner-up\n\\texttt{Bancroft} ($+36$ after $64$). The best two-character message\nis \\texttt{a`}: a $7$-digit seed ($4063723$) whose $16$-letter script\nthe automaton extends by $+34$ --- total $50$ letters from $22$ bits,\nratio $2.3$, i.e.\\ a survival champion wearing a two-character name.\nAnd the law is exact: best-of-$N$ search should win\n$\\log_{4/3}N$ forced letters --- prediction $37$ for the dictionary\n($N{=}44\\mathrm k$), $32$ for two characters ($N{=}9025$); measured\n$36$ and $34$. The dictionary behaves like $44{,}000$ random seeds:\nthere are no magic words, only Borel--Cantelli paying out at its\nstated rate.","old_string":"the automaton's, not yours, and (the\n$(3/4)^D$ law) a random message's orbit takes a letter $b\\ge3$ within\na handful of steps of being left to itself; try to compose a message\nwhose forced continuation survives long --- that is exactly the\nchampion-hunting problem, and the house always wins.","replace_all":false}